WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.920
Justice League unites DC's mightiest superheroes
in an epic battle to save the Earth from a

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.420
deadly threat, and the movie also features
not just one, but two cool end-credits scenes.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.001
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm breaking down what's in the

00:00:15.001 --> 00:00:19.769
Justice League post-credit scenes, what they
mean for the future of DC's cinematic universe

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:22.220
plus some hidden easter eggs you might have
missed.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.970
I'll be talking spoilers for the end-credits
only, not for the plot or the end of the movie.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.539
If you want to avoid any spoilers, tap here
for my spoiler-free video with the Justice

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:33.180
League cast at Comic Con.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:38.170
I'm also starting my giveaway for two Justice
League: The Art of The Film hardback books

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:42.400
which are loaded with concept art, sketches,
and behind-the-scenes shots from the set.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.280
For your chance to win, make sure you subscribe
and leave a comment about the movie on this

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.690
video.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.440
Let's start with the most important end-credits
scene which opens in a prison.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.359
There's a guard wearing a cap with an 'A'
for Arkham Asylum on it, and if you recall

00:00:57.359 --> 00:01:01.809
the end of Batman v Superman, this is where
Batman was sending Lex Luthor.

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:06.140
The guard calls over to a bald man in a cell
who has his back to the camera.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.640
When there's no response, the guard turns
the man around, revealing it's not Lex Luthor,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.470
but a another man who starts laughing maniacally.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.410
The scene then transitions to a boat arriving
at an extravagant yacht.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:21.220
Boarding the yacht, we see the silhouette
of a figure that comic book fans will instantly

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.450
recognise as Deathstroke, aka Slade Wilson.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.700
And he's meeting with none other than Lex
Luthor, who's evidently escaped from Arkham.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.530
Deathstroke then takes off his mask revealing
his signature eyepatch, plus grey-white hair

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.530
and beard.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.810
Lex Luthor says that with the Justice League
having just formed their own team of superheroes,

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.040
he and Slade Wilson need "to level the playing
field [and form] a league of [their] own."

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.280
So, this is hugely exciting because it means
we're looking at a supervillain team-up of

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.250
Luthor and Deathstroke, plus probably more
villains by the time the Justice League sequel

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:52.490
comes around.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.610
And there are two likely possibilities for
this supervillain league.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:02:00.140
The first is the Injustice League which, as
its name suggests, is a kind of opposite team

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.590
to the Justice League.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.840
Ezra Miller who plays The Flash has said he'd
like to see the Injustice League in Justice

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.170
League 2, although he's admitted he's not
party to DC's plans for the sequel yet.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.310
The second possibility is the Legion of Doom
and there are some hints that suggest this

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.940
could be DC's preferred option.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.890
On TV and in the comics, the Legion of Doom
has been a principal team of antagonists to

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.660
the Justice League, with Lex Luthor being
the leader in many versions.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:29.800
And Luthor clearly seems to be the initiator
of the idea in this end-credits scene, plus

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.620
as a billionaire he has all the obvious resources
to spearhead such a project.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.250
Most tellingly though is a tweet in early
October by the actor who plays Deathstroke,

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.510
Joe Manganiello, where he posted a picture
of himself wearing a shirt with "Legion of

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.590
Doom" on the front in a similar style to the
Justice League title.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:50.240
I also wonder whether the meeting between
Luthor and Deathstroke out on the water could

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.240
be a little hint to the Hall of Doom which
has been the Legion of Doom's headquarters

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.120
and is often hidden in a swamp.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.209
And, interestingly, in the "Final Crisis"
comic book storyline, the Hall of Doom is

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:05.300
offered to Lex Luthor and his team of supervillains
just before Darkseid's invasion of Earth.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.100
Incidentally, Zack Snyder already teased this
post-credits scene back in September 2016

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.740
when he tweeted a photo of a storyboard in
which we can see a picture of Deathstroke

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:17.140
talking to what appears to be a bald man.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.930
Doubtless, many fans will be very happy at
the prospect of Deathstroke coming to a DC

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:27.130
movie in the future, but Lex Luthor was quite
a divisive character in Batman v Superman,

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:31.970
with some fans not enjoying Jesse Eisenberg's
take on the megalomaniac supervillain.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.950
The post-credits scene is obviously very short
so it's hard to judge whether Eisenberg's

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.890
Luthor will engage a wider set of fans in
future movies, but the fact that he's out

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:45.020
of prison now also means he's available as
a villain in a potential Man of Steel 2.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:50.590
Deathstroke, aka Slade Wilson, is set to get
his own solo movie in the DC movie universe

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:55.210
and Warner Bros have been in talks with The
Raid director Gareth Evans to direct the film.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.450
So far we don't have details on whether the
solo movie might be a prequel of sorts to

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.160
his meeting with Lex Luthor, in other words
a Deathstroke origin story.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.660
But it wouldn't surprise me if that's what
it is.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:09.340
By the way, it's possible that the bald guy
in Arkham Asylum in the end-credits isn't

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.020
just a random criminal but it could be Mr
Zsasz, a Batman villain and serial killer

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:18.609
who carves a mark into his own body whenever
he kills someone, and who often appears with

00:04:18.609 --> 00:04:20.739
a shaved head.

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:25.069
The other end-credits scene is a fun one for
fans and features a Flash-Superman race.

00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:29.029
In the scene, Flash jokes that the race isn't
really a "macho measuring thing" to which

00:04:29.029 --> 00:04:32.360
Superman quips that if he wins Flash "is off
the team".

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.419
The race is a little homage to the discussions
many fans love to have about whether their

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:40.520
favourite character is faster or stronger
than someone else's favourite character.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.710
The scene doesn't really give us an answer
to this debate, ending in what looks like

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:48.229
a tie, in a similar outcome to some of the
Superman-Flash races in the comics.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:54.559
For example, in Superman #199, the UN proposed
a fund-raising race around the world between

00:04:54.559 --> 00:04:55.999
the two heroes.

00:04:55.999 --> 00:04:59.800
When Superman and The Flash discovered the
mob had set up illegal bets on the outcome

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:04.589
of the race they agreed on ending it with
a tie, a convenient outcome that ensured neither

00:05:04.589 --> 00:05:07.339
set of fans ended up disappointed.

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:13.360
And a similar scenario happened in The Flash
#175 when they raced around the entire Milky

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.360
Way.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.870
So, did the post-credits get you hyped for
Deathstroke?

